tions. Further experimentation yielded the distorted \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type \(M(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\) (\(M = \mathrm{La}, \mathrm{Ce}\)) through the reaction of \(M\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{LiBH}_{4}\) in toluene at room temperature, followed by extraction of \(M(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\) using dimethyl sulfide \(\left[\mathrm{S}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\right]\) to form a solvated phase. Subsequent loss of \(\mathrm{S}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\) from the adduct yields the \(M(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\) phase.\(^{202}\) The \(\mathrm{S}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\) templating strategy combined with halide-free reagents (rare earth hydride and \(\mathrm{S}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2} \cdot \mathrm{BH}_{3}\)) was then applied generally to generate new structures, include the cubic \(P a\bar{3} \cdot \alpha \cdot \mathrm{Pr}(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\) and \(\alpha \cdot \mathrm{Nd}(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\) structures,\(^{203}\) and \(\alpha \cdot \mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\), \(\beta_{2} \cdot \mathrm{Pr}(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\), \(\alpha \cdot \mathrm{Lu}(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\), \(\beta \cdot \mathrm{Pr}(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3}\).\(^{204}\) The latter paper also identified the structures of the \(\mathrm{S}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\)-adduct intermediates as layered phases, reminiscent of layered perovskites. Although the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\) phases are not direct precursors, the related \(\mathrm{Li}(M(\mathrm{BH}_{4})_{3})\mathrm{Cl}\) phases have potential application as fast Li-ion conductors for solid-state battery electrolytes and gas storage,\(^{205}\) with some lithium borohydride phases having been tested in devices.\(^{206}\)
Hypophosphites Until recently, the compound \(\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2})_{3}\) was the only example of a hypophosphite containing \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type compound,\(^{207}\) but following work by the authors here, it is now understood to be a member of a polymorphic family of \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type materials, \(M(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2})_{3}\), where \(M = \mathrm{Al}\), \(\mathrm{Ga}\), and \(\mathrm{V}\) [Figure 7 (b)]\(^{196}\) There are four polymorphs seen in this family, \((\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta)\), with each polymorph displaying different degrees of tilts and shifts of the \(M\) octahedra. Furthermore, each polymorph is preferred to varying degrees depending on the \(M\), with each \(M(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2})_{3}\) forming at least two of the four polymorphs. The high pressure (above 1 GPa) \(\delta\) phase has been observed for \(\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2})_{3}\), which normally prefers the less dense \(\alpha\) phase at STP. Synthetic control between each preferred polymorph is still
an open question, but to obtain at least one pure polymorph in each metal system, using an acid soluble oxide precursor is preferred (such as \(\gamma\!-\!\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) instead of corundum for the Al system). Interestingly, the compound \(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2})_{3}\), though its structure was unreported, is known to be an excellent bromine-free flame retardant in polymer blends.\[208\] As the structure was only just reported, there are potentially advantages to using one polymorph as opposed to another if synthetic control can be leveraged.
## 3 Metal-Organic Frameworks
Systems with Organic Linkers We have seen in the previous section that there are several inorganic systems with the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structure in which the anionic linkers are polyatomic. Such compounds present an interesting variation on the perovskite topology because the vacant \(A\)-site may create a space that is large enough to accommodate adsorbed solvent or small gas molecules. This is seen in the cyanides and the thiocyanates, though the cavity in the hypophosphites appears to be too small for even monatomic neon.\[196\] Since the cavities defined by the larger molecular \(X\) sites may even be large enough to deliver permanent porosity, there is a significant similarity between such systems and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). It is not surprising, therefore, to find that there are several examples of \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type materials in which the linker is an organic anion, and such materials can be properly regarded as MOFs. Examples of such systems, which are discussed below, include materials based on a wide variety of organic linkers, e.g. formates, azolates, guanidinates, and other, more complex ligands.
Formates, \( M^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3} \) \( M = \mathrm{Al} \), V, Fe, Ga, In \( ABX_{3} \) perovskite-type phases where \( X = (\mathrm{HCOO})^{-} \), formate, have been extensively studied as they are easily prepared using solution synthesis and show a wide range of interesting ferroelectric, magnetic, and multiferroic properties.\(^{209-211}\) The classical Goldschmidt tolerance factor equation,\(^{212}\) which predicts the relative radii of cations and anions that are compatible with simple inorganic perovskite formation, has been extended to hybrid perovskites\(^{213,214}\) and can be used to predict the sizes of the \( A \) cations that will stabilize the perovskite structure in systems such as \( AM^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3} \). Because the cavity is quite large, it is not surprising that quite large protonated amines, such as dimethylammonium, \( (\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+} \), are required. As an extension of this observation, it is also not surprising to find that \( \mathrm{ReO}_{3} \)-type systems of composition \( M^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3} \) have been reported, but only in the presence of neutral guest molecules that occupy the \( A \) site cavities.
A 1965 publication provided evidence that \( \mathrm{V}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3} \cdot \mathrm{HCOOH} \) may crystallize in the \( \mathrm{ReO}_{3} \) topology,\(^{215}\) but a full structure determination for this compound has not been performed to date. However, the powder diffraction pattern\(^{215}\) was indexed on a body-centred cubic structure that is consistent with \( M(\mathrm{III}) \) formates reported more recently,\(^{197}\) and other measures such as density are also close to the values reported for the Fe phase \( (\mathrm{V}^{3+} \) and \( \mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \) having similar molecular weights and radii). The neutral formic acid molecule, HCOOH, would be expected to occupy the \( A \) site.
The most recent work\(^{197}\) on the \( M^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{CHOO})_{3} \) systems \( (M = \mathrm{Al} \), Fe, Ga, In) confirmed
that these phases, which can be readily formed by solvothermal or reflux methods, adopt an \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structure with neutral \(A\)-site occupants assigned as combinations of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and HCOOH [Figure 7 (c)]. All of these phases crystallize in the cubic \(Im\bar{3}\) space group with \(a=11.4\,\mathrm{\AA}-12.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\). Unlike many of the other carboxylate MOFs, the molecules occupying the \(A\)-sites in the \(M^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{CHOO})_{3}\) systems could not be liberated while keeping the framework intact, suggesting that these molecules may have a structural role through strong hydrogen bonding or that the \(M-\mathrm{O}\) bonds are insufficiently strong to stabilize the framework in the absence of guests. An alternate form of the \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CHOO})_{3}\)·guest \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type phase was also reported,\(^{216}\) crystallizing in the trigonal \(R\bar{3}c\) space group with \(a=8.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\), \(c=22.6\,\mathrm{\AA}\). It exhibits antiferromagnetic ordering at low temperatures.
The formate \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type compounds could perhaps be regarded as the simplest MOF systems and can be made from inexpensive, earth-abundant starting materials. What is particularly interesting is that they show a very strong preference for forming in the presence of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3}\cdot x\mathrm{CO}_{2}/y\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) can be obtained by bubbling \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) through an aqueous solution containing \(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\) and formic acid.\(^{197}\) The fully stoichiometric phase, \(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3}\cdot\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), has yet to be achieved, but with a potential \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) loading of greater than \(21\,\mathrm{wt\%}\), it is an interesting option for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) storage.
Nitrogen-containing Organic Linkers In light of what has been reported for the formates, where the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type MOFs contain metal-oxygen bonds, it would be reasonable to expect that MOFs with the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structure might also form with nitrogen-containing
organic linkers that are capable of metal-nitrogen bonding. This has so far been realized for guanidinates, 1,2,4-triazolates, and imidazolates, though there are clearly other potential systems that might be feasible.
Only one guanidinate with the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structure is known to exist, \(\mathrm{Yb}[\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{3}]_{3}\).<sup>217</sup> This phase was synthesized in liquid ammonia and crystallizes in the cubic \(P n\bar{3}\) space group with \(a = 13.5\,\mathrm{\AA}\). Braced by the extensive hydrogen bonding between guanidinate anions, it has a relatively low density \((1.9\,\mathrm{g\,cm}^{-3})\) and potential voids in the structure. Unlike many of the simple inorganic \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type systems, \(\mathrm{Yb}(\mathrm{CN}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{4})_{3}\) shows positive thermal expansion rather than NTE. It is interesting to note that guanidine derivatives can play a role in perovskite-related materials both as an anionic linker (as in the present Yb phase) and as an \(A\)-site cation (as in the formate perovskites<sup>218</sup>). Clearly the control of pH is very important in determining which of these roles it is able to play.
While azolates represent a well-known building block for MOFs (particularly ZIFs, where imidazolates are used as linkers), they are primarily used in tetrahedral coordination with \(M(\mathrm{II})\) cations. Nevertheless, there are a small number of \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structures based azolates. \(\mathrm{Yb}(\mathrm{tz})_{3}\) \((\mathrm{tz} = 1,2,4\)-triazolate) was prepared from Yb metal and a melt of the azole, crystallizing in the cubic \(P m\bar{3}m\) space group with \(a = 6.8\,\mathrm{\AA}\).<sup>219</sup> \(\mathrm{Yb}(\mathrm{tz})_{3}\) is arguably not strictly an \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type as half of the tz ligands coordinate \(\eta_{1}\) while the other half coordinate \(\eta_{2}\), giving ytterbium a coordination number of nine; however, the overall topology is \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-like. This phase is well-packed and shows no potential porosity. \(\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{trz})_{3}\)
(trz = 1,2,4-triazolate) was later reported by the same group,\(^{220}\) forming at the interface between the immiscible liquid metal and liquid triazole. This phase crystallizes in the cubic \(I23\) space group with \(a = 12.3\,\mathrm{\AA}\), and gas sorption testing showed that \(\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{tz})_{3}\) is also a dense framework with no accessible porosity. We are not aware of any \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structure based on 1,2,3-triazolate linkers.
Most recently, imidazole (im) was successfully used to form \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{im})_{3}\cdot x G\) (\(G =\) guest) \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type phases via multiple pathways including ionothermal, solvothermal, and solventless syntheses.\(^{221}\) Three phases were obtained - I, II, and III - with structural parameters of \(Im\bar{3}\), \(a = 13.0\,\mathrm{\AA}\); \(R\bar{3}\), \(a = 18.9\,\mathrm{\AA}\), \(c = 10.5\,\mathrm{\AA}\); and \(R\bar{3}\), \(a = 9.1\,\mathrm{\AA}\), \(c = 22.3\,\mathrm{\AA}\), respectively [Figure 7 (d)]. While gas sorption showed no accessible porosity, varying amounts of unbound neutral molecules appeared to be trapped inside the framework cavities of phases I and II. Phase III was reported as a completely dense, guest-free structure. All structures were solved from PXRD, and the identities and quantities of neutral guest molecules in I and II were assigned by NMR to a range of molecules including solvent and solvent decomposition products.\(^{221}\) The structures were distinguished by different octahedral tilting patterns. Phases I and II showed a reversible, discontinuous, hysteretic phase transition at ca. \(90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The synthesis method affected the transition temperature, and it was suggested that this was due to the different guest molecules trapped within the framework. It would seem unlikely that \(\mathrm{In}^{\mathrm{III}}\) is the only trivalent cation capable of forming \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type networks with imidazolate, so this seems to be a very interesting area for future exploration.
Figure 8: Triply interpenetrating MOF based on a bis-imidazole ligand. Figure was adapted with permission from Abrahams et al..222
Systems with More Complex Linkers and Interpenetration There are several examples of positively charged \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type MOF frameworks with large organic linkers and charge-compensating anions in the \(A\)-site cavities,\(^{223}\) but these are more like perovkites than \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\) systems (albeit with inverse polarity). They are sometimes referred to in the MOF literature as having the \(\alpha\)-polonium structure type (\(\alpha\)-Po being the only simple cubic metal structure), irrespective of the framework being charged and the presence of anions in the \(A\)-site cavities. An important example is provided by the large, neutral bis-imidazole ligands (Figure 8), which forms \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type frameworks with \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) nodes.\(^{222}\) What is particularly interesting is that the frameworks are triply interpenetrating, presumably to reduce the unoccupied space in the channels. Such behaviour is also seen in some of the MOF-5 related materials discussed below. The positive charges of the frameworks in these structures are balanced by nitrate or tetrafluoroborate anions which sit in the small cavities created by the interpenetrating \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\) nets. One would imagine that neutral frameworks of
this type could be obtained by using a trivalent cation in combination with anionic ligands.
MOFs with Polyatomic \( B \)-site Cations In this final section on MOFs with the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structure, we shall focus on an even more complex group of materials in which the cations on the \( B \)-site are polyatomic clusters, again linked by organic ligands. The most iconic example is MOF-5, shown in Figure 9, where tetrahedral \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{6}^{+}\)clusters are connected by 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (1,4-bdc) anions to form a 3-D network of composition \([\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}](1,4-\mathrm{bdc})_{3}\) [Figure 9 (a)].<sup>17</sup> This important discovery from Yaghi's group lead to another concept known as reticular synthesis,<sup>224,225</sup> whereby structures with even larger cavities could be obtained by using longer linkers, as shown in Figure 9 (b). By so doing, it is possible to tune the porosity of the MOF system for specific applications. This family of MOFs is, therefore, justifiably famous for being a model for the optimization of porous frameworks for applications in separation and catalytic processes.<sup>226</sup> We note that interpenetration is often found in structures based on the longer linkers used to synthesize some of the IRMOF materials, though steps can be taken to mitigate this outcome.
There can be endless variations on the MOF-5 theme if we allow for the possibility that the 1,4-bdc linker might bear substituents on the benzene rings. Typical examples include \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{Br}\), \(-\mathrm{Cl}\), \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\).<sup>228</sup> Yaghi refers to such systems as multivariate MOFs on account of the range of compositional variations that are possible, such as combining eight distinct functionalities into a single phase. The chemical variations are not of solely academic interest, since it is possible to tune the adsorption behaviour of the MOF for partic-
Figure 9: (a) The structure of the metal-organic framework, MOF-5, with a \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\) unit on the \(M\)-site, 1,4-bezenedicarboxylate anions on the \(X\)-site, of an \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\) topology. This structure type is referred to as pcu (primitive cubic) in the MOF community. Atom legend; zinc = light blue, oxygen = orange, carbon = brown, hydrogen = white. (b) Isoreticular series of MOFs, where IRMOF-1 = MOF-5. Panel b was from Rosi et al.227
ular applications. In one instance, the selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption compared with \(\mathrm{CO}\) could be enhanced by \(400\%\) by fine-tuning the composition.\(^{228}\)
There are symmetry constraints on the types of systems that can form an \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type network, since the building unit at the \(B\)-site node must be compatible with 6-fold connectivity, as discussed in a review by Zaworotko et al.\(^{229}\) This is achieved in MOF-5 by using the two oxygens from one end of the 1,4-bdc carboxylate linker to bond to two different zinc ions in the cluster. In spite of this constraint, there are many other examples of systems that adopt the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structure (which is often referred to as the pcu - primitive cubic - topology in the recent MOF literature, though this appellation is sometimes interpreted more loosely than we have done in this review). Eye-catching examples include a system in which the nodes use \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{4}\) paddlewheel clusters as vertices.\(^{230}\). To give a sense of the potential scope of this area, we note that Snurr and Hupp explored the methane storage and delivery capacity of 122,835 hypothetical pcu frameworks\(^{231}\) from the MOF database of Wilmer et al.,\(^{232}\) though the vast majority of these have never been synthesized.
There are many other MOFs in the literature that are described as PCU-type that we have not included here because they do not preserve the cubic or pseudo-cubic architecture of the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structures. Typically they have different linkages along different axes, such as the pillared-layered examples\(^{233,234}\) and other structures based on paddlewheel nodes.\(^{235}\)
## 4 Concluding Remarks
The chemical diversity of materials with the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type structure is much greater than we had imagined when we began to think about this article in the summer of 2018. Furthermore, their properties and applications, both actual and potential, have also surpassed our expectations. The timeline shown in Figure 2 highlights some of the important systems that have been developed over the last eighty-plus years, from their initial discovery or structural characterization to the demonstration of their most striking properties. In addition to the remarkable range of their behaviour, we are struck by the long gaps between the initial discoveries of several of the materials and the establishment of their functionalities. In the case of \(\mathrm{ScF}_{3}\), for example, seventy years elapsed between the first structural characterization in 1939\(^{236}\) and the recognition of its outstanding NTE behaviour in 2010! At the same time, several new \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type families of been reported in the last decade, including the borohydrides, hypophosphites, guanidinates and imidazolates, though the properties of these new families have yet to be explored.
One of the most exciting discoveries in the very recent past concerns a 2015 report that hydrogen sulfide, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), transforms to \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{S}\) under high pressure and that this new phase is superconducting with a Tc of 203 K.\(^{237}\) This possibility was predicted in 2014\(^{238}\) and subsequent work in 2016 has indicated that the superconducting \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{S}\) phase adopts a doubly interpenetrated \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\) structure.\(^{239}\) However, others have claimed that the superconducting phase is \((\mathrm{SH}^{-})(\mathrm{SH}^{3+})\) and adopts the perovskite structure with \((\mathrm{SH}^{-})\) on the \(A\)-site,\(^{240}\) and
recent calculations broadly support the idea of such a disproportionation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\).<sup>241</sup>
We noted at the beginning of the article that most of the \(ABX_{3}\) perovskite families can form \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\) analogues, and vice versa, so it is appropriate at this point to revisit this issue and identify the systems where this has not been established. Table 4 shows a number of cases where the analogues are not known, or even where neither form is known but could potentially exist. In some cases, such as the \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\) form of \(\mathrm{BiI}_{3}\), the compound prefers a layered structure, which is presumably stabilized by strong van der Waals interactions between the iodide ions. In the case of the hypothetical \(A^{I}B^{II}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\), it might be that the protons that protrude into the \(A\)-site cavity render it unsuitable for hosting a metallic cation. However, there are several nitrogen-containing linkers that might lend themselves to the formation of new \(\mathrm{ReO}_{3}\)-type or perovskite families, though we note that some of these may be high energy materials, such as the azides and the tetrazoles. Future work in these areas is certainly needed and has the potential to produce new phases with interesting and unusual properties.
